who says that all main characters are  heroic yet we know our heroes to often  be paragons of justice  often winning through the power of  positive feelings friendships and  determination but that's not very fun is  it  not realistic to boot what if we can  have main characters that instead follow  human nature  and channel the power of evil well it's  not as dramatic as that  but there are quite a few anime out  there where our main characters  protagonists or otherwise  aren't exactly shining examples of  people who have the moral high ground  still it gives them a required edge to  keep the story going in the not so  typical direction  plus evil is cool isn't it right okay  i've talked enough  it's time to go through the top 10 anime  with villainous main characters  [Music]  starting things off we have black lagoon  living a monotonous life rokuro must  have been waiting for some divine  intervention to help spice up his daily  trudge through the boring every day  well divine intervention did come just  not in the form that anyone would have  prayed for i mean who'd pray to have  their lives changed in the form of  capture by a mercenary group calling  themselves black lagoon  circumstances then force him to  cooperate with this squad that makes his  living by less than virtuous means it's  truly survival of the fittest as rokuro  finds himself needing to adjust to this  hellhole he finds himself in black  lagoon is a high  octane thriller that seeks to dive into  themes like morals and virtue  it's an exciting look at rokuro's  character as his worldview begins to  transform  thanks to his less than moral crew i  mean surrounded with a cast full of  jerks  psychotic and outright evil characters  who wouldn't do so  if you've seen clips of revi who's one  of the main characters you know exactly  what i'm talking about  survival of the fittest is the name of  the game in such a crap sack world after  all  in eighth place is gankutsuo i don't  know about you but i just love it when  classic  literature and anime intersect and what  better piece of classic  literature to adapt into anime than one  of the most lasting and memorable tales  of revenge  the count of monte cristo now take such  a beloved tale and add some sci-fi  elements into it and you get  gankutsuo the main character being  immoral is pretty much a given if you're  familiar with the source material i mean  it's a tale of cold-blooded revenge for  crying out loud on the other hand i'd  like to bring your attention to  gankutsuo's art style i don't think that  i ever saw  anything like this before picking this  show up it surely divided the anime  community a bit when they first saw it  but whatever your reactions to that are  please don't let it hamper your  enjoyment when watching this creative  and innovative retelling of one of the  most beloved literary classics out there  ever heard of that grandpa who gets  caught in an explosion and comes out of  it a hero  well here it is inuyashiki feels the  full wrath of an explosion and comes out  of it reformed as  a cyborg while he decides to use his  powers for good  he also catches wind of another person  who's gone through what he has  the villainous shishigami now i'd say  that inuyashiki is an interesting case  for this list because honestly in most  regards our old man protagonist  is heroic unlike most of the immoral  protagonist or protagonist  allies that get featured in the shows  i've been talking about hinuyashiki  himself is  generally a good guy what i want to  focus on here is shishigami shishigami  is the villain but he's the main  character and a lot of the story is told  from his perspective  this opens up some angles to which the  story can be told and i really like how  it was done  in inuyashiki it gives you a certain  perspective that you don't expect from  what seemed to be a superhero show  inuyashiki is not a long anime so i  think that you can give it a shot if  you're in for some no-holds-barred  action featuring discussions on two  characters seated on  very opposite ends of the moral spectrum  all hail the almighty irons in our  number six show  overlord have you heard of the exploits  of the amazing ein summer once a  powerful wizard and a master of his own  guild in the vr game idracil mamonga  decides to  enjoy his last hours in the online world  just as it crumbles  only it doesn't and he gets stuck in the  body of his skeleton lich avatar  left in a world after the end he now  finds himself the master of a completely  different world  one where non-player characters seem to  have awakened to personalities of their  own now christen the overlord of this  new world momonga eventually eins olgon  begins his conquest  at his beck and call our loyal servants  of the tomb of monsters that will help  him find the answers to this predicament  that he finds himself in  overpowered protagonist anime done right  overlord sure has garnered its fair  share of polarizing opinions from its  execution  its world and its characters but being  someone who enjoyed irregular at magic  high school and the currently airing  misfit  of demon king academy i just can't help  but be a sucker for overpowered  protagonists that give  no damn about morals i do think a nice  aspect to overlord is just seeing how  the humanity in iron slowly erodes as he  continues his journey  i do also think that the other  characters deserve more shine but  reveling at the awesomeness of the  protagonist kept me in the ride with  that you more or less have an idea of  what to expect in overlord so go ahead  and see for yourself if it's to your  liking  remember that anime starring terrorist  protagonists well it's here at number  five  terror in residence terror in resonance  finds tokyo in the midst of a chain of  terrorist attacks by our protagonist duo  dubbing themselves sphinx they continue  the attacks leaving behind riddles as  their way of looking for challenging  opponents  the efforts do pay off attracting a  particular individual  kenjiro shibazaki now the battle is on  between the terrorists and the genius  set to end the duo's reign of terror the  themes of terror in resonance might be a  little bit unsettling for others but i  think that having such  extremists as protagonists opens up a  rarely explored angle in anime  terror in resonance is rich in themes  that deal with politics  morals and the like it's also rife with  tragedy and mysteries surrounding the  main duo and how we reach this  particular point  if you're up for a show that helps  reshape your understanding of the world  terror in resonance is here  fourth on the list is yujo senki  localized as  saga of tanya the evil doesn't the  pretty ominous title already sound you  off  well you're in for a hell of a ride what  happens when a remorseless hr manager  gets issacied and dropped into the  heartless midst of a war-torn land  well take her immoral skills and vicious  streak in the real world and translate  it into war of course  such is the story of tanya a protagonist  in yu josenki  tanya's every bit cold-hearted immoral  calculating and utterly ruthless  it doesn't mean that the show can't be a  fun ride especially seeing all the crazy  [ __ ] she pulls  turns out that the merciless world of  warfare is just the perfect place for a  hr manager to run wild  this crazy setup comes back with some of  the best background art and soundtrack  from recent anime that i can recall  and i can see why the show became such a  big hit if you enjoy anime with a unique  plot  are a sucker for wartime settings in  strategic plots or simply want to root  for intelligent and calculating main  characters  yujosenki could be the show just for you  91 days brings us to the prohibition era  quite the stark contrast to the  pseudo-world war world of yujo senki  here we get acquainted with angelo  lagoosa who has his own quest for  revenge against the vanities  i think it's a no-brainer as to why the  title makes it onto this list  you've got to toss aside some morals to  pursue such a quest after all the  opening scene alone establishes what  kind of protagonist we have and i think  that makes him all the more of an  interesting character to follow  personally i think that 91 days is a  show that was mostly carried by how  charismatic the main character is  and how interesting the writing of him  is the show itself is more of a slow  paced show that builds up  excellently to its climactic act the  slow burn star might not be for everyone  but those who follow through will find  themselves audience to one of the best  revenge dramas that anime can offer  it's got suspense it's got a lot of  intrigue and it has some of the most  engaging characters  found in the genre  [Music]  i was really really torn with who to  give the first place spot to  but narrowly missing out the top spot by  an inch is code geass  where can we get started with code geass  give the most magnificent  megalomaniacal genius in the world with  the power to control minds  and witness the fireworks of political  upheaval  oh and throw in some cool robots there  and some of the greatest art and  animation the late 2000s has seen  and you've got what could be the 2000s  anime of the decade  chess master just doesn't do enough  justice to describe the cerebral  abilities of the characters in this show  and it was such a joy watching the  battle of wits week after week with the  rest of the anime community back then  lelouch also isn't bound by a lot of the  morals that hold back a lot of anime  heroes so we can see the depths and full  capacity of the human mind through him  it's really telling to see how much of  an impact code geass has made  decades after its cathartic conclusion  you still see a huge fan base and a lot  of additional material heck you still  see a lot of collabs with random mobile  games out there  i do think that the only thing it lacked  is a little bit more mainstream  penetration that the number one placer  on this list had  at least locally i'd argue that in terms  of actual show quality though  code geass might as well be the number  one show on our list  so any guesses as to what comes next  on the top of the list is a suspiciously  similar show  both in terms of scope popularity and  the sheer magnificence of its  protagonist  it's death note who hasn't heard of  death note  i think it's even more of the  quintessential villain protagonist show  even more than code geass as i think  lelouch is overall just a more  sympathetic character than light yagami  for those who don't know death notes  basically what happens when you give an  arrogant hero with a god complex the  complete power to enact justice  using the titular death note light  begins his crusade to rid the world of  crime by wiping out criminals one at a  time with his handy power  as we all know absolute power corrupts  absolutely and we eventually get to some  of the best games of cat and mouse  between him and super detective  l i think it's a no-brainer that death  note gets to the top of our list  seriously what other series on this list  had as much of a lasting  impact as death note i think only code  geass which missed out on the top spot  by a hare's length can compare but given  how much of a good social commentary on  justice and morality death note was and  still is  its legacy doesn't surprise me one bit  so how's that for a lineup of shows with  immoral main  characters do these shows prove or  disprove the point that edgy is cool  tell me your opinions on the mentioned  shows that you've watched and i'm sure  that we'll have a good time talking  about your not so typical main  characters  as of now i'll take my leave with 10  shows that will help you ponder on your  world views and morals  that's plenty of time to keep you busy  until the next video  [Music]  you
 

